      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let the center of the hexagon be at the origin, and let the sides of the hexagon be parallel to the imaginary axis, such that the vertices of the hexagon are of the form \\(re^{i\\theta_k}\\) for \\(k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\). The distance between opposite sides is one unit, so the side length of the hexagon is \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\).\n\nThe vertices of the hexagon can be written as:\n\\[ z_k = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\left( \\cos k\\pi + i\\sin k\\pi \\right) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\]\n\nThe reciprocal of \\(z_k\\) is:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{z_k} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1} \\left( \\cos(k\\pi) - i\\sin(k\\pi) \\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\left( (-1)^k - i(-1)^k \\right) = (-1)^k \\sqrt{2}(1-i) \\]\n\nThe region \\(R\\) outside the hexagon consists of the set of all points \\(w = \\frac{1}{z}\\), where \\(z\\) is a point in the complex plane outside the hexagon. The region \\(S = \\left\\lbrace\\frac{1}{z} \\ | \\ z \\in R\\right\\rbrace\\) is therefore the region inside the hexagon, because the reciprocal of a point inside the hexagon is outside the hexagon.\n\nThe area of a regular hexagon with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2 \\]\nFor our hexagon with side length \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\), the area is:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\]\n\nThus, the area of \\(S\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}} \\]",
