      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by identifying the vertices of the regular hexagon and understanding its geometric properties. A regular hexagon inscribed in the unit circle has vertices at the sixth roots of unity, which can be written as:\n\\[ 1, \\omega, \\omega^2, \\omega^3, \\omega^4, \\omega^5 \\]\nwhere \\(\\omega = e^{i\\pi/3} = \\frac{1}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nThe hexagon is centered at the origin and the opposite sides are one unit apart, meaning the distance between any two adjacent vertices (which are \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega^5\\)) is two in the complex plane. This implies that the side length of the hexagon is \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) and the distance between the center and a vertex is 1.\n\nThe area of the regular hexagon can be calculated using the formula for the area of a regular polygon:\n\\[ \\text{Area of the hexagon} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{perimeter} \\times \\text{apothem} \\]\nThe perimeter of the hexagon is \\(6 \\times \\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\\) and the apothem is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times 2 = \\sqrt{3}\\). Therefore, the area is:\n\\[ \\text{Area of the hexagon} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6\\sqrt{3} \\times \\sqrt{3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 18 = 9 \\]\n\nThe region \\(R\\) is the set of all points outside this hexagon. To find the region \\(S = \\left\\lbrace \\frac{1}{z} \\mid z \\in R \\right\\rbrace\\), we need to find where the reciprocal of a point outside the hexagon maps back inside the hexagon. The reciprocal of a point inside the hexagon lies inside the hexagon, but the reciprocal of a point outside the hexagon lies outside a smaller hexagon with the same center but a side length of \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-2} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), which is equivalent to \\(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) in terms of the unit circle.\n\nThe area of the new hexagon is:\n\\[ \\text{Area of the new hexagon} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times \\sqrt{3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\(S\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{9}{2}} \\]",
