    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the geometry and properties of the hexagon. A regular hexagon centered at the origin with side length \\(1\\) has vertices at \\( \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\pm \\frac{1}{2}i \\). The distance between opposite pairs of sides is two of these side lengths, which is \\(2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 1\\).\n\nThe region \\( R \\) is the area exterior to this hexagon. The region \\( S = \\left\\lbrace \\frac{1}{z} \\mid z \\in R \\right\\rbrace \\) is the inversion of \\( R \\) with respect to the unit circle. The inversion transformation \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps an exterior region to an interior region of a specific shape.\n\nFor a regular hexagon, the inversion with respect to the unit circle maps each point \\( z \\) to \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\). The region \\( S \\) will be a regular hexagon with center at the origin and opposite pairs of sides one unit apart, but now formed by the inversion of the original hexagon.\n\nThe side length of the original hexagon is 1. After inversion, the side length of the new hexagon will be \\(\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\) (by the properties of inversion). However, the area of the hexagon is preserved under inversion, and the area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\) is given by \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2 \\). For the original hexagon, the side length is 1, so the area is \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\nFor the inverted hexagon, the side length is \\(\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\), but the area remains the same because inversion preserves area. The area of the inverted hexagon is therefore the same as the area of the original hexagon, which is \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{3\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
